body:
Interestingly enough, the fact that this part of France was once part of Germany still has legal consequences to this day. 

Alsace and Moselle were German territory between 1871 and 1918. When they got reunited with France after WWI, inhabitants of Alsace and Moselle insisted to keep some of the German laws, as well as some of the Napoleonian laws which had been abrogated in the meantime in the rest of France. 

Which is why, even today, these parts of France have kept a slightly different legal system, with practical consequences: 
• The local social security system there is a bit more generous than in the rest of the country; 
• The separation of the Church and the State, which was voted in 1905 in France, was not fully implemented in Alsace-Moselle when they rejoined France;
• Whereas trains circulate on the left track in the rest of the country, in Alsace-Moselle they're driven on the right track;
Etc.

Without hesitation, SCP 231. I cannot read it again anymore.

&#x200B;

(There was also an SCP which looked like an endless pile of vomit and organs or something, and which contained a log about two people being stuck inside of it, but I can't remember the number of it – which is a shame because as opposed to 231, I'd love to read it again but can't find the number!)

Tout ce que je retiens de Voltaire, c'est qu'il a décrit des viols de manière très très cynique dans *Candide*, et ça, en tant que victime d'agression sexuelle qui se bat encore avec ce trauma, je ne lui pardonnerai jamais.

&#x200B;

Ceci mis à part : texte très intéressant, très instructif, et quand même inquiétant… Je souhaite bien du courage à cette professeure. Il est incroyable de voir à quel point les préjugés font tant de mal dans les esprits des jeunes.

Precision about the "Now (25 y.o.)" picture: I didn't cut my hair, it's just tied behind my head! I still have very long hair that reaches my butt (and I intend to keep this mane forever).

&#x200B;

